{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS CloudTrail launches Insights for data events to automatically detect anomalies in data access - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail Insights, a feature that helps identify and respond to unusual activity in AWS accounts, has been expanded to include data events. Previously, Insights analyzed only management events, but now it also monitors data events, enhancing the ability to detect potential security or operational issues. Insights for data events automatically detects anomalies in data access activities, such as increased delete Amazon S3 object API calls or error rates for AWS Lambda function invocations. This feature establishes normal baselines for data access patterns and alerts users when it detects anomalies, providing the relevant data events from the anomaly period for precise investigation. Users can configure alerts for potential issues and respond rapidly to threats or issues. Insights for data events is available in all regions where AWS CloudTrail is available, and additional charges apply for this feature. To get started, users should refer to the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/cloudtrail-insights-data-events-detect-anomalies-access/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS CloudTrail adds data event aggregation to simplify security monitoring - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service that records API calls for AWS account activity, introduces a new feature called CloudTrail aggregated events. This feature simplifies monitoring and analysis of high-volume CloudTrail data events by consolidating them into 5-minute summaries. Security, compliance, and operations teams can efficiently identify trends like access frequency, error rates, and frequently used actions without processing individual events. Aggregations are available for API activity, resource access, and user activity summaries. Users can enable aggregation through the AWS console or CLI and choose from pre-built templates. The aggregation service is charged based on the number of CloudTrail data events analyzed. It is available in all commercial AWS Regions. With this feature, teams can answer questions like \"How has this user's activity changed over the past week?\" or \"What are the top actions being performed on this critical resource?\" without having to scan through massive amounts of CloudTrail data events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/cloudtrail-data-event-aggregation-security-monitoring/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-observability","skill_description":"Investigates root causes on the Omni context graph (service dependencies) and Intelligence insights; builds, configures, debugs, tunes AWS observability - operator-symptom questions, detecting Omni vs classic CloudWatch. CloudWatch on a reporting service: fleet health views, Log Insights, metric/composite/anomaly alarms, custom metrics/EMF, dashboards, X-Ray/ADOT tracing, canaries, CloudTrail, Dynamic Instrumentation (live breakpoints), and Application Signals service map. CloudWatch Omni on an existing Space: SQL over logs/traces, PromQL, Omni dashboards, alerts and Slack notifications, programmatic/IaC access (API/SDK/CLI/CFN), driving Omni from a coding agent or skills, AI agent quality from traces - on-demand and online evaluation of live traffic, incl. setting it up, readback, evaluators. For first-time setup - creating an Omni Space, granting access, ingestion, or ADOT instrumentation for Application Signals or Omni - use setting-up-cloudwatch-observability. Not for app logging or threat detection.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, cloudwatch-omni, xray, cloudtrail, synthetics, application-signals\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, optimize, configure, enable, onboard, instrument, query\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability\n*","skill_name":"aws-observability"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudTrail Event history limitations for audit reliance."}
